steps.text:
When you compare one value against many constants, `switch` is tidier than a long `else if` chain:

```c
switch (key) {
    case 'w':
        printf("forward\n");
        break;
    case 's':
        printf("back\n");
        break;
    default:
        printf("?\n");
}
```

Each `case` needs a `break;`, otherwise execution **falls through** into the next case. `default` catches everything else.

**Your turn:** read a char and print the direction for `w`, `a`, `s`, `d` (`forward`, `left`, `back`, `right`), or `unknown` for anything else.

steps.seed:
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    char key;
    scanf(" %c", &key);
    [[switch]] (key) {
        case 'w': printf("forward\n"); [[break]];
        case 'a': printf("left\n"); break;
        [[case]] 's': printf("back\n"); break;
        case 'd': printf("right\n"); break;
        [[default]]: printf("unknown\n");
    }
    return 0;
}

steps.solution:
#include <stdio.h>

int main(void) {
    char key;
    scanf(" %c", &key);
    switch (key) {
        case 'w': printf("forward\n"); break;
        case 'a': printf("left\n"); break;
        case 's': printf("back\n"); break;
        case 'd': printf("right\n"); break;
        default: printf("unknown\n");
    }
    return 0;
}

