steps.text:
A very common pattern: one pointer starts at the front, one at the back, and they walk toward each other until they meet.

```c
int *lo = a, *hi = a + n - 1;
while (lo < hi) {
    /* work with *lo and *hi */
    lo++;
    hi--;
}
```

Comparing two pointers with `<` is fine when both point into the same array.

**Your turn:** write `void reverse(int *a, int n)` that reverses the array **in place** using two pointers (no `[ ]`).

steps.solution:
void reverse(int *a, int n) {
    if (n < 2) return;
    int *lo = a, *hi = a + n - 1;
    while (lo < hi) {
        int tmp = *lo;
        *lo = *hi;
        *hi = tmp;
        lo++;
        hi--;
    }
}

steps.harness:
int main(void) {
    int a[] = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5};
    reverse(a, 5);
    CHECK("odd length", a[0] == 5 && a[1] == 4 && a[2] == 3 && a[3] == 2 && a[4] == 1);
    int b[] = {10, 20, 30, 40};
    reverse(b, 4);
    CHECK("even length", b[0] == 40 && b[1] == 30 && b[2] == 20 && b[3] == 10);
    int c[] = {8};
    reverse(c, 1);
    CHECK_INT("one element", c[0], 8);
    reverse(c, 0);
    CHECK_INT("zero elements does nothing", c[0], 8);
    return 0;
}

steps.solution:
void squeeze(char *s) {
    char *w = s;
    for (const char *r = s; *r != '\0'; r++) {
        if (*r != ' ') {
            *w = *r;
            w++;
        }
    }
    *w = '\0';
}

steps.harness:
